  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nToday through tomorrow night...\n\nIncreasing mid to high level clouds with some embedded high'lofted \nshowers are beginning to develop over the region, which is funneling \nup from the transverse banding associated with Hurricane Marie, \nwhich is now located roughly just over 700 miles to the southwest of \nSan Diego. This will continue to track towards the northwest slowly \nand continue to usher in more mid level moisture ahead of a longwave \ntrough upstream and with some forcing, which will result in better \ndevelopment of bands of light to moderate rain. The best convergence \naloft will be located over the San Bernardino Mountains, with the \nhighest probability of measurable rain, and possibly an embedded \nthunderstorm or two. Most of the lighter showers that are currently \nover San Diego County this morning will transition further north by \nlater in the day. Given the timing and position of where these bands \nof light to moderate rain establish themselves in a southwest to \nnortheast orientation. Total rainfall amounts may reach possibly \nover an inch for the the San Bernardino Mountains, and then tapering \nto lesser amounts from around a half an inch for the higher terrain \nof the mountains in Riverside/northern San Diego counties. \nRainfall amounts will generally be 0.20 to 0.30 inches within the \nIE, with slightly lesser amounts to the coastal areas of Orange \nCounty, and drastically tapering off to lesser amounts over San \nDiego County (with possibly some areas over southern San Diego \nCounty receiving little to no precip). These bands of rain will \neventually dissipate by later in the evening. Temperatures will \nremain on the cooler side today, around and slightly below the \nseasonal average for this time of year, with extensive cloud cover\nover the region. For tomorrow, temperatures will rebound back to \naround average, with drier conditions and only a very slight \nchance of an afternoon shower or thunderstorm over the mountains \nand adjacent deserts.\n\nTuesday through Friday...\n\nDrier conditions will return by Tuesday, with most of the moisture \nassociated with Hurricane Marie exiting the region, and only some \nlingering moisture being advected up some the south which may allow \nfor a slight chance of a afternoon shower or storm to develop over \nthe mountains. As the ridge continues to build into the middle of \nthe week, with will warm temperatures back above the seasonal \naverage, with the warmest day peaking on Wednesday, which will find \nhigh temperatures returning back into the low to mid 90s for the \ninland areas. Wednesday will also bring a better bout of mid level \nmoisture being advected in from the east, with a better chance of\nafternoon showers and thunderstorms returning for the mountains \nand adjacent deserts, along with a gradual cooldown throughout the\nrest of the work week. The marine layer will likely also continue\nto be displaced and patchy throughout this next week.",
